Title: "Named Function Calls"
Id: "f787a585-0a72-496d-9060-b82bbfd57e42"

Text: "A named function call supplies a function's arguments by name using `[| ... |]`.

```mlg-fragment
f[|value := x, index := n|]
f[|value = x, index = n|]
```

Each entry has a left-hand side, then `:=` or `=`, then the argument expression.
Both spellings are accepted; `=` is the current form and `:=` the legacy one.

The left-hand side is a name, an inferred parameter name, a subset call, a
ranged mapping selector, or the catch-all `...`:

```mlg-fragment
f[|value := x, Pair[left, right] := y, nested[outer[inner]] := z|]
f[|x1?_ = 1, x2?_ = 2, ... = 0|]
f[|x_[i_[1...m]] = 1, ... = 0|]
```

The catch-all slot `... = value` supplies the value for every parameter no
earlier element names. Omitting it represents a **partial application**, such as
`f[|x1?_ = 1|]`.

The names identify which parameter each value fills; the call renders like an
ordinary positional call, so `f[|value := x, index := n|]` renders as `f(x, n)`.
This is distinct from a function literal, which uses `=>`."
Id: "c3a40261-4ef1-4014-8016-1c201655dcef"
